    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some test data for a new brake assembly using ceramic-matrix composites. During high-speed deceleration tests, we're seeing some erratic thermal spikes that don't quite align with our heat soak models. Can you explain the primary differences in how ceramic vs semi-metallic pads manage heat dissipation during these rapid thermal cycling events?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic matrix composites generally offer superior thermal stability and lower heat capacity compared to semi-metallic pads, allowing them to manage high temperatures without significant softening or warping during rapid cycles. Semi-metallic brakes dissipate heat more aggressively through friction-generated airflow but are prone to thermal shock at extreme rates, often leading to the erratic spikes you observed. In contrast, ceramic materials maintain their structural integrity and heat transfer characteristics more consistently, which usually prevents the sudden performance degradation seen in standard composite formulations. To address your data discrepancies, you may need to account for the unique transient thermal conductivity of the CMC and how its relative heat soak differs from traditional pad chemistry under these specific deceleration profiles.",
      "source": "target",
